WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.900
There are different kinds of polyglots.

00:00:01.924 --> 00:00:05.133
There are polyglots who like
to read and listen

00:00:05.157 --> 00:00:09.100
and get into the culture
of different languages.

00:00:09.124 --> 00:00:13.000
And then, there are
the irrepressible travelers,

00:00:13.024 --> 00:00:18.900
extroverts who naturally, quickly learn
to speak a variety of languages

00:00:18.924 --> 00:00:22.567
comfortably and are very enjoyable
to be around.

00:00:22.591 --> 00:00:25.567
One of these is my next guest,
Gabriel from Brazil.

00:00:25.591 --> 00:00:29.200
I'm sure you're going to find
this interview very interesting.

00:00:29.303 --> 00:00:30.912
Gabriel, nice to see you again.

00:00:31.421 --> 00:00:33.317
Likewise, Steve.
It's a great pleasure.

00:00:33.341 --> 00:00:38.479
And it's been so long since the last time
we got together in person, it's crazy.

00:00:38.503 --> 00:00:42.745
And we got together in person, because you,
for a lot of time, lived in Vancouver.

00:00:42.769 --> 00:00:46.645
We used to see each other in Vancouver.
And now, I believe here, in Paris.

00:00:46.669 --> 00:00:51.452
And your origins are from Brazil, and so,
you are quite a globe trotter,

00:00:51.477 --> 00:00:53.381
as well as an accomplished polyglot.

00:00:53.405 --> 00:00:57.979
What I want to talk to you about today
is something that I was thinking about.

00:00:58.712 --> 00:01:02.212
As I was actually doing some Danish,
and then, I said,

00:01:02.237 --> 00:01:05.312
"Okay, now I got to talk to Gabriel
in Portuguese."

00:01:05.336 --> 00:01:08.279
And I never did much in Portuguese.

00:01:08.303 --> 00:01:10.145
I'm gonna be really bad.

00:01:10.169 --> 00:01:11.345
And then, I said to myself,

00:01:11.369 --> 00:01:14.345
"So, for those of us who would like
to explore different languages,

00:01:14.369 --> 00:01:17.879
how do we maintain them or do we even
worry about maintaining them?"

00:01:17.903 --> 00:01:21.445
"And to what extent do we lose them?"
And that's your thing.

00:01:21.469 --> 00:01:24.512
And you speak, I don't know,
10 to 12 languages or so, right?

00:01:24.536 --> 00:01:26.812
At varying levels, or is it more?

00:01:27.312 --> 00:01:30.312
Well, I would say 11.

00:01:30.336 --> 00:01:33.112
At 11, that I would say-

00:01:33.137 --> 00:01:40.645
I would be comfortable to say that
I came close to fluency, at least,

00:01:40.669 --> 00:01:44.545
or I was fluent at one point or another.

00:01:45.945 --> 00:01:48.312
And I've dabbled in a lot more.

00:01:48.336 --> 00:01:52.012
But I don't really count those languages,
because, you know, just like-

00:01:52.036 --> 00:01:54.445
In many of them, I just reach maybe A2.

00:01:54.469 --> 00:01:56.469
Or maybe B1.

00:01:56.493 --> 00:02:01.312
And in a lot of them, I'm still at A1,
I just got a taste of the language.

00:02:01.336 --> 00:02:06.912
And I put them aside in order to focus
on the languages that are more comfortable.

00:02:06.936 --> 00:02:08.845
So, I would say about 11.

00:02:08.869 --> 00:02:14.979
Okay, so, would you agree that,
to learn a language to the level of A1,

00:02:15.003 --> 00:02:19.379
or A2, or B1, or as you put it,
to taste the language,

00:02:19.403 --> 00:02:21.945
is an activity that is well worthwhile?

00:02:23.045 --> 00:02:24.745
For me, it has been.

00:02:24.769 --> 00:02:28.679
It's always been really fun
to just get started.

00:02:28.703 --> 00:02:34.345
Specially because one of the main reasons
why I do this is to interact with people.

00:02:34.369 --> 00:02:40.645
So, go to international parties,
say a few things in whatever language.

00:02:40.669 --> 00:02:43.445
For example,
Japanese is a language that I love,

00:02:43.470 --> 00:02:47.012
that I want to learn seriously
in the future,

00:02:47.036 --> 00:02:51.412
I think I may have reached
the equivalent of A2, right?

00:02:51.436 --> 00:02:57.479
But I'm aware that to reach fluency,
that's going to take,

00:02:57.503 --> 00:03:02.412
you know, a lot of time and dedication,
and it would take me years to accomplish.

00:03:02.436 --> 00:03:05.112
So, it's something that I put aside,

00:03:05.136 --> 00:03:11.245
because I want to get to the equivalent
of C1 in Mandarin before I do that.

00:03:11.445 --> 00:03:14.279
But it's really rewarding, anyway.

00:03:14.303 --> 00:03:20.054
It's fun to just be at A1 or A2 in Japanese
just to say and understand a few things.

00:03:20.079 --> 00:03:22.012
And I really like anime.

00:03:22.036 --> 00:03:26.312
So, I like to watch anime
and just understand some things.

00:03:26.336 --> 00:03:28.336
And it's all fun.

00:03:28.712 --> 00:03:30.579
I mean, I think that's
an important message.

00:03:30.603 --> 00:03:34.712
If people learning languages are exploring
two or three or more languages

00:03:34.736 --> 00:03:39.179
feel that if they don't achieve B2
at least, then somehow, they're a failure.

00:03:39.203 --> 00:03:42.245
I think that's a very bad way,
a very sort of-

00:03:42.269 --> 00:03:44.579
I would say a discouraging way
of looking at things.

00:03:44.603 --> 00:03:47.945
Even in those languages
that I never got very far in,

00:03:48.545 --> 00:03:51.379
I obviously know a lot more about
those languages,

00:03:51.403 --> 00:03:53.212
have a better sense of those cultures,

00:03:53.236 --> 00:03:56.879
than if I had never, ever looked
at the language.

00:03:56.903 --> 00:04:00.679
So, I think it's well worthwhile,
you know,

00:04:00.703 --> 00:04:03.279
even getting to that limited level
in a language.

00:04:03.303 --> 00:04:06.745
And then, if that spirit moves,
you just move on to something else.

00:04:06.769 --> 00:04:08.879
- Yes.
- Is that kind of your approach?

00:04:08.903 --> 00:04:09.917
Absolutely.

00:04:09.942 --> 00:04:13.312
I think it's so cool to get
a taste of the language, right?

00:04:13.336 --> 00:04:15.212
Just like you said before.

00:04:15.812 --> 00:04:19.912
There are languages that I dabble in,
a lot of Slavic languages like,

00:04:19.936 --> 00:04:24.679
Czech, Croatian, Serbian, as well as-

00:04:24.703 --> 00:04:28.245
Not right now,
I'm actually doing a 30-day challenge

00:04:28.269 --> 00:04:31.379
to learn as much Ukrainian
as I possibly can.

00:04:31.912 --> 00:04:34.845
And it's really cool, right?

00:04:34.869 --> 00:04:40.679
Specially since I speak some decent
Russian, it makes it easier.

00:04:40.704 --> 00:04:43.945
And it's really interesting to see
all the differences.

00:04:44.512 --> 00:04:48.045
And you start understanding a bit more
of all of them at once in a way.

00:04:48.069 --> 00:04:51.945
So, when the languages are connected,
it's really fun to do that.

00:04:51.969 --> 00:04:55.779
But yeah, absolutely.
I agree with you that it's all fun.

00:04:56.012 --> 00:04:59.945
Now, here's another sort of issue.

00:04:59.969 --> 00:05:03.545
And that is, do you do anything special
to maintain your languages?

00:05:03.569 --> 00:05:08.612
Not the ones that are A1,
I find those the quickest to fall away.

00:05:08.945 --> 00:05:12.979
But once you're almost at a B2 level,

00:05:13.003 --> 00:05:15.545
do you do anything special
to maintain them?

00:05:16.679 --> 00:05:19.179
I would say so, but-

00:05:19.203 --> 00:05:23.679
You know, there are languages
 like German that I reached C1.

00:05:25.512 --> 00:05:29.545
I don't think I reached C2 in German,
just because I haven't really needed it.

00:05:29.569 --> 00:05:34.212
So, I just prefer to focus on different
languages and try to get to B2.

00:05:35.679 --> 00:05:39.779
So, I go through phases in which
I just neglect German,

00:05:39.803 --> 00:05:41.645
and I don't get a lot of exposure.

00:05:41.669 --> 00:05:45.479
So, I do try to get some exposure,
and eventually,

00:05:45.503 --> 00:05:50.579
from doing videos in the streets of Paris,
I meet German people, so I talk to them.

00:05:50.603 --> 00:05:52.603
And I know I may interview them.

00:05:53.345 --> 00:05:57.479
So, it's like a nice way to just try to,
you know, keep it going.

00:05:57.503 --> 00:06:03.112
But since it's a high enough level
of German,

00:06:03.136 --> 00:06:06.812
I can just basically put it aside,
forget about it for a while,

00:06:06.836 --> 00:06:09.345
and I won't forget that much, anyway.

00:06:09.369 --> 00:06:15.679
But I'll still be far from where I was
when I was dating a German girl.

00:06:15.703 --> 00:06:18.812
I was going to Germany all the time,
and reading a lot of Nietzsche

00:06:18.837 --> 00:06:21.679
and Kafka in German, right?

00:06:22.012 --> 00:06:26.545
Okay, let me say a few things
to those people listening or watching,

00:06:26.569 --> 00:06:29.512
who don't know what B2, C1, etc. means.

00:06:29.536 --> 00:06:31.536
Essentially, A is beginner.

00:06:31.560 --> 00:06:32.712
B is intermediate.

00:06:32.736 --> 00:06:33.845
C is advanced.

00:06:33.869 --> 00:06:38.645
And each one of those is divided
into A1-A2, B1-B2, C1-C2.

00:06:38.669 --> 00:06:42.012
And this is the common European framework

00:06:42.036 --> 00:06:46.045
which sort of tries to establish
what your level in the language is.

00:06:46.069 --> 00:06:50.445
I think most of us who say,
"I speak this to a B1 or B2 level."

00:06:50.469 --> 00:06:52.679
Have never been tested,
I have never been tested.

00:06:52.703 --> 00:06:55.145
However, it's a handy way of describing

00:06:55.169 --> 00:06:57.169
what you think your level
in the language is

00:06:57.193 --> 00:07:00.879
or your level of confidence,
of your ability in the language is.

00:07:00.903 --> 00:07:02.512
I think- Certainly, that's my case.

00:07:02.536 --> 00:07:06.512
I don't know, have you taken tests
to confirm that you're B1 or B2?

00:07:06.779 --> 00:07:13.879
For French, I got the
Diplôme approfondi de langue française.

00:07:15.012 --> 00:07:17.112
I got the B2 back in 2011.

00:07:17.136 --> 00:07:19.445
Okay, so you have taken the test.

00:07:19.469 --> 00:07:21.912
I have taken that one and I passed.

00:07:22.679 --> 00:07:25.579
With a terrible grade, by the way,
but I passed.

00:07:26.645 --> 00:07:28.512
But I'd say that-
So, in French-

00:07:28.536 --> 00:07:34.179
You know, right now, I'm living in Paris,
so I'm definitely C1.

00:07:34.203 --> 00:07:37.212
I don't know, I don't think I'm at C2 yet.

00:07:37.236 --> 00:07:40.079
Because I've also not been focusing
that much on French.

00:07:40.103 --> 00:07:44.379
I've been focusing more on
Mandarin and Russian.

00:07:44.403 --> 00:07:48.679
And right now, for instance,
I'm entirely focused on Ukrainian.

00:07:50.512 --> 00:07:55.679
But, for example, for German,
I would be very confident that I'm at C1.

00:07:56.079 --> 00:07:59.445
But yeah, I haven't bothered
going to the institute.

00:08:00.179 --> 00:08:02.312
Yes, we have to be very careful
whenever we say that.

00:08:02.336 --> 00:08:06.012
It's not like I got a BA from Oxford
or something like that.

00:08:06.036 --> 00:08:08.545
We have not, in most cases,
have been tested.

00:08:08.569 --> 00:08:12.479
It's just a handy way,
it's something a lot of people understand.

00:08:12.503 --> 00:08:16.912
To me, B2 is what I call fluency,
which means I can speak comfortably.

00:08:16.936 --> 00:08:20.479
The person listening to me
is comfortable listening to me.

00:08:20.503 --> 00:08:24.979
We communicate, I make mistakes,
I understand most things.

00:08:25.003 --> 00:08:29.912
To me, that's kind of the functional goal
when I go to learn a language.

00:08:29.936 --> 00:08:33.645
But very often, I don't achieve that,
I may never get there,

00:08:33.670 --> 00:08:36.545
but it doesn't take away from my
enjoyment of discovering the language.

00:08:36.945 --> 00:08:40.979
And I think people listening to you
or even to me might say,

00:08:41.003 --> 00:08:43.412
"Geez, I could never learn
so many languages."

00:08:44.212 --> 00:08:46.545
I think it's a matter of-

00:08:46.569 --> 00:08:50.779
Once you start doing it, you discover
one language helps you learn another.

00:08:50.803 --> 00:08:52.803
I think a lot of people are-

00:08:52.827 --> 00:08:57.445
You know, potentially,
if that was their hobby like it is for us,

00:08:57.469 --> 00:09:00.545
they're capable of learning
many languages.

00:09:00.569 --> 00:09:02.745
But most people struggle with even one.

00:09:02.769 --> 00:09:07.512
And I think most of the advice you give
at your channel, if I'm not mistaken,

00:09:07.536 --> 00:09:09.879
is for Portuguese speakers,

00:09:09.903 --> 00:09:12.512
and potentially speakers
of other languages,

00:09:12.536 --> 00:09:15.112
how they can learn English better.

00:09:15.136 --> 00:09:18.712
Because that's the one most people
are trying to learn.

00:09:18.736 --> 00:09:23.545
And a lot of those techniques we use
for learning a variety of languages

00:09:23.569 --> 00:09:27.079
apply just as well for people
who are trying to learn one language.

00:09:27.103 --> 00:09:29.779
While we are on the subject
of maintaining different languages,

00:09:29.803 --> 00:09:32.012
let's switch to Portuguese,
but let's just keep going.

00:09:32.036 --> 00:09:33.712
Okay, that's good.

00:09:33.736 --> 00:09:37.279
And it's a language that I never used,
a language that I never really got

00:09:37.303 --> 00:09:40.712
that good a grasp on,
because my Spanish interferes.

00:09:40.736 --> 00:09:43.679
But I don't worry about it.
People understand me, I understand them.

00:09:43.703 --> 00:09:46.379
[Portuguese] Good morning, everyone.

00:09:46.403 --> 00:09:50.412
Today, I will be speaking in Portuguese
with my guest-

00:09:50.436 --> 00:09:52.445
Do you say "invitado" for "guest"
in Portuguese?

00:09:54.445 --> 00:09:56.645
How do you say, "guest"?

00:09:57.312 --> 00:09:58.879
I forgot, my-

00:09:58.903 --> 00:10:02.879
- My friend, Gabriel.
- My friend.

00:10:03.379 --> 00:10:07.279
He's a great Brazilian polyglot
who can speak-

00:10:07.303 --> 00:10:09.745
Not more languages than I can.

00:10:09.769 --> 00:10:11.412
No.

00:10:11.436 --> 00:10:13.245
And he likes to learn languages,

00:10:13.269 --> 00:10:20.245
he's always learning new languages
with great enthusiasm.

00:10:20.679 --> 00:10:22.269
Today, we'll be talking about-

00:10:22.293 --> 00:10:27.445
And I will leave a, how do you say,
a "link" or an "enlace"?

00:10:27.469 --> 00:10:28.879
We say "a link."

00:10:28.903 --> 00:10:32.912
A link to Gabriel's channel.

00:10:33.645 --> 00:10:41.079
I want to talk about this concept
of learning the basic things in a language,

00:10:41.104 --> 00:10:53.445
and how can we learn to master
the basic things in a language,

00:10:53.469 --> 00:11:02.645
and up to what extent is this concept
of mastering the basics is realistic.

00:11:03.959 --> 00:11:06.279
First of all, I just remembered
the word we forgot.

00:11:06.303 --> 00:11:08.245
I sometimes forget Portuguese words, too.

00:11:08.269 --> 00:11:11.586
It's "Convidado."

00:11:11.611 --> 00:11:16.479
- I'm forgetting all about Portuguese.
- I forgot it, too. But it's fine.

00:11:16.579 --> 00:11:19.145
Yes, Steve.
I think it's a very interesting subject.

00:11:19.169 --> 00:11:23.445
Especially because,
just like we talked about many times,

00:11:24.812 --> 00:11:32.012
far too often, there is a large focus
in learning the basics

00:11:32.036 --> 00:11:38.612
in lessons or certain language courses,
especially the more traditional ones.

00:11:38.636 --> 00:11:43.020
So, what can we really do to

00:11:43.045 --> 00:11:45.612
master the basics of a language
or skip the basics altogether?

00:11:45.636 --> 00:11:48.112
Especially because, just like we said-

00:11:48.979 --> 00:11:51.745
If we're learning,
"Names of bathroom items."

00:11:52.312 --> 00:11:54.679
"Names of the colors in a language."

00:11:55.312 --> 00:11:57.979
"Names of vegetables in grocery stores."

00:11:58.003 --> 00:12:01.912
"Names of animals in the zoo"
and such, right?

00:12:01.936 --> 00:12:03.712
So, personally, I think-

00:12:03.736 --> 00:12:09.179
I myself sometimes use
a stronger word for a few things.

00:12:09.203 --> 00:12:13.979
I usually say a high vocabulary for a low
frequency is useless in the beginning.

00:12:14.003 --> 00:12:15.712
Unless-

00:12:15.736 --> 00:12:20.812
Unless we really have
a specific use for it, right?

00:12:20.836 --> 00:12:23.179
If we want to use, for example,
what I just mentioned.

00:12:23.203 --> 00:12:26.945
Really, if you work at a zoo
and you work a lot with certain animals,

00:12:26.969 --> 00:12:30.745
you would need to use that vocabulary,
there's no helping it.

00:12:30.769 --> 00:12:33.112
If you work at an airport,
you would have to talk-

00:12:33.136 --> 00:12:35.979
You would probably have a conversation
at the customs, I don't know.

00:12:36.312 --> 00:12:41.312
But I think it's very important
to learn things in context.

00:12:41.745 --> 00:12:42.779
Right?

00:12:42.803 --> 00:12:46.479
Exactly, but there are a few basic things

00:12:49.479 --> 00:12:54.912
that are really basic,
but really hard to learn.

00:12:54.936 --> 00:13:00.212
You still need a lot of time
to assimilate these things.

00:13:00.236 --> 00:13:03.879
But there are also things
that are not as basic.

00:13:04.179 --> 00:13:08.279
Like, for instance, those bathroom items.

00:13:08.745 --> 00:13:12.212
The fruits in a grocery story and all that,
the name of the colors.

00:13:13.145 --> 00:13:19.645
Things that are easy for people
who write books about language learning,

00:13:19.669 --> 00:13:25.645
that are easy for teachers,
but that are not basic.

00:13:25.669 --> 00:13:28.445
The names of the colors, the numbers,
those aren't basic.

00:13:28.469 --> 00:13:37.579
And these are things, just like you said,
that you learn through context,

00:13:37.603 --> 00:13:40.412
context we hope is interesting, right?

00:13:40.436 --> 00:13:48.112
So, far too often, the students,
the people who learn languages,

00:13:48.136 --> 00:13:55.245
they get frustrated,
because they forget about the basic things.

00:13:55.269 --> 00:13:58.012
I always tell them, "That's normal."

00:13:58.036 --> 00:14:01.112
"You will keep forgetting these things."

00:14:01.136 --> 00:14:07.212
"And only after much time has passed,

00:14:07.236 --> 00:14:11.412
your brain will gradually
get used to these things."

00:14:12.412 --> 00:14:20.078
So, the concept of mastering or learning,
deliberately learning the basic things,

00:14:20.103 --> 00:14:25.245
is a concept that's wrong
in language learning.

00:14:25.812 --> 00:14:29.645
Yes, no doubt.
I think that's a very important message.

00:14:29.879 --> 00:14:34.145
Because I think many students won't even
necessarily understand that initially.

00:14:34.445 --> 00:14:36.612
Because usually, when I-

00:14:36.636 --> 00:14:41.045
For example, let's suppose I say,
"We'll learn the Passé composé in French."

00:14:41.069 --> 00:14:48.020
"So, let's hammer that down in our heads,
'je suis allé', 'nous sommes allés',

00:14:48.045 --> 00:14:52.245
'j’ai été', which is what we use.
Let's keep hammering that down."

00:14:52.269 --> 00:14:58.512
Just like you said, deliberately learning
isn't going to be extremely effective.

00:14:59.045 --> 00:15:05.012
We're going to make 300 mistakes,
we'll forget all about the verb être

00:15:05.036 --> 00:15:07.679
or the verb avoir, we'll forget.

00:15:07.703 --> 00:15:11.479
We'll forget about the conjugation,
and we'll get upset.

00:15:11.503 --> 00:15:14.945
Only through a huge level of exposure,

00:15:14.969 --> 00:15:18.411
and only by making mistakes
and hearing the correct form,

00:15:18.436 --> 00:15:24.712
is that we'll gradually start to really
assimilate and master this-

00:15:24.736 --> 00:15:26.736
This thing we're learning.

00:15:26.760 --> 00:15:28.979
But what I think is most interesting
as well is that-

00:15:29.003 --> 00:15:32.079
Okay, maybe we can be introduced to it,
maybe someone can explain it to us,

00:15:32.103 --> 00:15:33.779
"Look, this is how it works."

00:15:33.803 --> 00:15:38.212
And we can even do some grammar exercises
or something like that,

00:15:38.236 --> 00:15:39.512
if the person wants to.

00:15:40.512 --> 00:15:43.945
However, in order for us
to really put that in practice,

00:15:43.969 --> 00:15:47.679
we'll need a huge level of exposure.

00:15:47.703 --> 00:15:51.431
Well, for example,
it's also interesting that, everyone-

00:15:51.456 --> 00:15:55.479
Someone who speaks English
as their mother tongue.

00:15:55.503 --> 00:15:59.279
The order of the adjectives,
I think it's very interesting,

00:15:59.303 --> 00:16:03.212
because people aren't even aware of it.

00:16:03.236 --> 00:16:08.712
For instance, if someone says,
"That is a big black shoe."

00:16:10.145 --> 00:16:16.279
If someone says that in reverse,
"Black big shoe."

00:16:16.303 --> 00:16:20.179
That will sound really strange
to a native speaker.

00:16:20.203 --> 00:16:23.879
And many native speakers won't even know
why does that sound so strange.

00:16:23.903 --> 00:16:26.679
They will correct you, they'll tell you,
"No, it's 'big black shoe.'"

00:16:26.703 --> 00:16:29.612
What I mean is,
everyone is already teaching that.

00:16:29.636 --> 00:16:32.445
You'll have to hammer that order down.

00:16:32.469 --> 00:16:37.245
"No, first of all comes the color,
then it has to be this, then it's that."

00:16:37.679 --> 00:16:42.579
And really, with a high level of exposure,
after hearing it so many times, so often,

00:16:42.603 --> 00:16:48.045
that's when we'll naturally start to know
what is the correct order of everything.

00:16:49.045 --> 00:16:50.645
- What do you think, Steve?
- Exactly.

00:16:50.669 --> 00:16:56.579
Exactly, these things about conjugation
exist in several different languages.

00:17:01.579 --> 00:17:04.912
So, it's very-

00:17:04.936 --> 00:17:10.045
For a person who speaks English
as their mother tongue,

00:17:10.069 --> 00:17:13.745
it will be very difficult to learn
this concept of conjugation.

00:17:13.769 --> 00:17:18.845
And there's also gendered words, right?
Is it "gênero", male and female?

00:17:18.869 --> 00:17:22.279
If you try to learn the rules,

00:17:22.303 --> 00:17:25.612
"Which words are female
and which words are male?"

00:17:25.636 --> 00:17:27.479
That's very, very difficult.

00:17:27.503 --> 00:17:33.245
Only through a lot, but a lot of exposure.

00:17:33.269 --> 00:17:37.944
A lot of- How do you say it?
A lot of reading and listening,

00:17:37.969 --> 00:17:41.879
is that the brain will get used to it.

00:17:41.903 --> 00:17:45.812
So, yes.
Is it possible? Maybe.

00:17:47.345 --> 00:17:53.612
There's nothing wrong with reading
an explanation about the grammar,

00:17:53.636 --> 00:17:58.145
but that won't guarantee
that you will learn those things.

00:17:58.169 --> 00:18:03.745
Only through continuous reading,
continuous use in conversations,

00:18:03.769 --> 00:18:07.979
continuous listening to what the other
person with whom you're talking is saying-

00:18:08.003 --> 00:18:12.679
Gradually, it's a slow progress.

00:18:13.412 --> 00:18:18.645
But you need to have faith in your brain,

00:18:18.669 --> 00:18:25.079
which, with enough input,
as we call it in English-

00:18:25.103 --> 00:18:30.179
The brain can't make
our minds want to learn.

00:18:30.203 --> 00:18:36.845
The brain is a learning machine,
but that's a slow, slow process.

00:18:36.869 --> 00:18:38.612
Slow, slow, slow.

00:18:38.636 --> 00:18:45.879
So, we don't need to get frustrated
because we forgot about the basics,

00:18:45.903 --> 00:18:52.012
because we can't make a conjugation
in a language like Portuguese or French,

00:18:52.036 --> 00:18:55.412
because we don't know the gendered words,
because we keep making mistakes.

00:18:55.436 --> 00:19:02.712
Making mistakes and all that is normal,
that's part of the learning process.

00:19:03.212 --> 00:19:05.245
Undoubtedly.
This reminds me of something very-

00:19:05.269 --> 00:19:08.479
I'll mention really quick
something you mentioned

00:19:08.503 --> 00:19:14.379
in a video about Stephen Krashen's
theories, I believe.

00:19:14.403 --> 00:19:18.279
- About the order of-
- The great mentor.

00:19:18.303 --> 00:19:24.145
And something that fascinates me a lot is,
logically, how many students expect

00:19:24.170 --> 00:19:28.045
that there is a logical order of things
that we need to learn in a language.

00:19:28.069 --> 00:19:31.845
And now, something that-
Far too often, what'll happen is,

00:19:31.869 --> 00:19:34.979
he has a theory about
the existence of a natural order

00:19:35.003 --> 00:19:38.679
that we can't force, nor change.

00:19:38.703 --> 00:19:41.712
And something that fascinates me is,
for instance,

00:19:41.736 --> 00:19:48.212
English students often learn
Gerunds on a continuum.

00:19:48.236 --> 00:19:51.312
For example,
"I am going, am doing something."

00:19:51.336 --> 00:19:56.345
And that's a much more complex concept
than a simple conjugation of verb "to do."

00:19:56.369 --> 00:19:59.712
For example,
"I do, you do, they do, he does."

00:19:59.736 --> 00:20:05.345
And look, for instance,
I know many students and Brazilian friends,

00:20:05.369 --> 00:20:08.512
and even people who have lived in Canada
or the United States for years,

00:20:08.536 --> 00:20:14.379
who get this basic conjugation wrong,
"He do" or "They does."

00:20:14.403 --> 00:20:15.745
Don't know, something like that.

00:20:15.769 --> 00:20:20.012
And I mean, it's something that'll take
longer than some more simple concepts.

00:20:20.036 --> 00:20:21.645
So, it's something we can't force on them.

00:20:21.669 --> 00:20:25.379
Only through a lot of exposure
is that we'll be able to absorb that.

00:20:26.145 --> 00:20:27.579
Right, yes.

00:20:27.603 --> 00:20:34.445
And so, the conclusion is that
we need to keep at it, keep studying

00:20:34.469 --> 00:20:42.345
and enjoying the learning process
without doubting yourself.

00:20:42.369 --> 00:20:44.045
How do you say that in your language?

00:20:44.069 --> 00:20:45.379
"Sem duvidar."

00:20:45.403 --> 00:20:47.403
Yes, without doubting yourself.

00:20:47.427 --> 00:20:51.779
And speaking while making mistakes
isn't a huge deal.

00:20:51.803 --> 00:20:56.712
The most important is understanding
and continuing to speak.

00:20:56.736 --> 00:20:59.645
And gradually, you'll get better at it.

00:20:59.669 --> 00:21:02.345
Undoubtedly.

00:21:02.369 --> 00:21:06.712
Well, let's stop it here.

00:21:06.736 --> 00:21:12.379
And thank you very much,
it's always a big pleasure for me, Gabriel.

00:21:12.403 --> 00:21:15.679
And as we say in Spanish,
"Mucha suerte."

00:21:16.445 --> 00:21:20.145
Thank you very much, Steve.
It's always a pleasure talking with you.

00:21:20.169 --> 00:21:22.169
- A big hug.
- See you soon.

